{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-73574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/a266f4d3ab4254684c91cb422f96b70a4c9973f587b6afb2cd7af5db45e86561.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $z=4-x-y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-73574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/cd218d4c4ef62d63ae2954c0974fb25a95257d9fc322834b0dec4aae3e8e99fc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-73574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-73574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-73574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-73574" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}$\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=-2x-2y+6$ onto $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2y+6 & =8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+({y}^{2}-2y+1) & =4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=4$ centered at $(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 4}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $z$ is \n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x-2y+6,$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,y)=8-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161160" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between  the plane $z=2y+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le x\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=2y+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$\n- $a=1$ and $b=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,y)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=2y+1$ and *above* by $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{{2y+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,y)}\\le z\\le {{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2y+1$ onto the $xy$-plane. \n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+2y+1) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle ${x}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=4$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xy$-plane.    \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/39d2daceb171d26d3396e5aaeab4d8b8da2e9b8324e4042f2b7ed048f4a5fdeb.png)\n\nThe region is bounded on the *left* by $x=-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}$ and bounded on the *right* by $x=\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,      \n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{4-(y+1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=-3$ and bounded *above* by $y=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-3}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II  and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161143" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/7bb6d2b9f9e09de44e078657ce3517bfd78261d3206743b7882abf0112f38a93.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $y=0$, $y=1$, $z=0,$ and $z=3-x+y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Indeed, we have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=3-x+y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/65e179e16b66009b9168c4380032b01c3a79b3c4a2afc3b8e5084defb77b85fc.png)\n\n \n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161117" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e5369264a9ab9328449bafd6fd3dcda1a70b7d90cbaf4f9815211e0cc3873fc0.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=3$, $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0,$ and $4x+5z=15.$ Given its representation\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {f}_{2}(x,y)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xy$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II and statement III are false. We have\n\n- the *bottom* surface $z={f}_{1}(x,y)=0$, and\n\n- the *top* surface $z={f}_{2}(x,y)=\\frac{15-4x}{5}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/edea8af334c3362f583fcb86709150690eeec79a54185b67d242bfa14a8e4c75.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161125" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2y$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=0$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=1-{y}^{2}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $x=2y,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{2y}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/01cb7f25e98c8607258f673a5c5be6a1e55869b379c4484c05a04cebbe5dc5f6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160965" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(y)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(y),\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$\n- ${g}_{1}(y)=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$\n- $a=-1$ and $b=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type II representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(y,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z).$ Since all $x$ in the region are bounded *below* by $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and *above* by $x=\\sqrt{2y},$ we have\n\n$$\n{{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(y,z)}\\le x\\le {{\\sqrt{2y}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(y,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto the $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the cone is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/d6db5f2d27787c5414f92c2525398b3810d0b0d192ea6837f7cdf483531f48e9.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(y)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(y-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(y)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $y.$ From the diagram, $y$ is bounded *below* by $y=0$ and bounded *above* by $y=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le y\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-162530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/d294519b3909517b9507322c86ad970550ab388a8d759b0ada99ed18627fc4bd.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=2$, $z=0$, $z=1$, $x=0,$ and $2x+y=6.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=6-2y$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=\\frac{6-y}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/487b57182e190cbacf3b1ad30f55d227b59fb470e8b5609ad72327d229e53e45.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-168955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $x=\\sqrt{2y}.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/98b9dc84e3d23d43c1fef204109d6f6b6c571d6f188592f1f08bb1abf4dc3641.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, and statement II is true. \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the cone $x=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}$ and the paraboloid $x=\\sqrt{2y}$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the paraboloid is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the cone, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{2y} \\\\ {y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+1+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(y,z)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},$ and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(y,z)=\\sqrt{2y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/60f622b161dbfb4da1d18e85d3eb3ab4e02c2af418f9845e05a3bf9704a28006.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$, $x=0,$ and $x=2+y-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(y,z)\\le x\\le {f}_{2}(y,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=2-y-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(y,z)=2+y-z$ and ${f}_{2}(y,z)=5$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type II region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $yz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are false. We have\n\n- the *back* surface $x={f}_{1}(y,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *front* surface $x={f}_{2}(y,z)=2+y-z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c2af4029bd979d250ed15f351ef7f14d3ae688806db4ffec199a24b3591dadac.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/40091bf7a66577a4a3f3b8cdf3b5da34cbe0e0198fdc5731c6d3dae8cb727418.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x=0$, $x=1$, $z=0$, $z=3$, $y=0,$ and $y=4-z.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=0$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=4-z$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The projection $D$ of the solid onto the $xz$-plane gives the domain \n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. We have\n\n- the *left* surface $y={f}_{1}(x,z)=0$, and\n\n- the *right* surface $y={f}_{2}(x,z)=4-z.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/b1046e312a3783dfc3253646f8a71ae226a173ac882a6305850bdd81aed9dd7e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161248" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}\\le 3}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and *above* by $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{2x}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto the $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/0bcadeca7ede6f709bf06391e10da66afd1b514fd0e041bbf108e6e4229b0da6.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{0}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{2}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is true.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-160962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(2x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2z+1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2z-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =2 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=1$ centered at $(0,1)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the following ellipse:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,2{x}^{2}+(z-1)^{2}\\le 2}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2z+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,{g}_{1}(x)\\le z\\le {g}_{2}(x),\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$\n- ${g}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and ${g}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$\n- $a=0$ and $b=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type III representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces ${f}_{1}(x,z)$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z).$ Since all $y$ in the region are bounded *below* by $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ and *above* by $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{{2{x}^{2}+2z-1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{1}(x,z)}\\le y\\le {{-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{2}(x,z)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y=-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1$ and the parabolic cylinder $y=2{x}^{2}+2z-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+2z-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+2z-2 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+({z}^{2}+2z+1) & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=3$ centered at $(0,-1)$ in the $xz$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/868fceefed2a426feebe2a9199279826aaee935963fec8fa3761b17b97542c34.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $z=-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}$ and bounded *above* by $z=-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1-\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{1}(x)}\\le z\\le {{-1+\\sqrt{3-3{x}^{2}}}_{⏟}}_{{g}_{2}(x)}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=-1$ and bounded *above* by $x=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{{-1}_{⏟}}_{a}\\le x\\le {{1}_{⏟}}_{b}.\n$$\n\nSo, statement III is false.\n\nFinally, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e1a7c108cbe2fbffcdc116880ed85e216e2357c3aa04977355c2c9be9c9acb06"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "q-161227" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/state.json" "file_sha256" "1611f0c90b85bad4e556d83371584ec9bdef4ee14e61c9a0d28d45c3f30a201b"} {"question" "e-6175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x.$ Express $R$ as a type I region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: a \\leq x \\leq b,  \\quad g_1(x) \\leq y \\leq g_2(x), \\quad f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6175.json" "file_sha256" "40d902111a59c6613298d2b602bdb751830834b2fae7f41a083d14dd10db663a"} {"question" "e-6175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/05f940e247c6cd5d862d81fc3a34658353bef5513dfe862bbc67a120c0345838.png)\n\n \n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces $f_1(x,y)$ and $f_2(x,y).$ Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by $z=x^2+y^2$ and *above* by $z=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+y^2}_{f_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq  \\underbrace{2x}_{f_2(x,y)} .\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z.$ Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x - 1)^2 +  y^2 = 1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/2c16cae9e18867b3fa0d61203a6a91b9944e6595fa15bfd8d3b424346fefabee.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by $y=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}$ and bounded *above* by $y=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_2(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x.$ From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_a \\leq x \\leq \\underbrace{2}_b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $R$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2,  \\:\\:-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2} \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}, \\:\\: x^2+y^2 \\leq z \\leq 2x\\right\\}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6175.json" "file_sha256" "40d902111a59c6613298d2b602bdb751830834b2fae7f41a083d14dd10db663a"} {"question" "e-6176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y.$ Express $R$ as a type II region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6176.json" "file_sha256" "45e4028c159d10b4fb3561152013805cc004b4803ef14c652fe5e3aea360101e"} {"question" "e-6176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/e4c9cbbad2f13e9cc125ddbb89125ed2d734eee3f80737125961f4af1846a972.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x.$ Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & ={y}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-2y+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2y+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(y - 1)^2 +  z^2 = 1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: (y - 1)^2 +  z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the front and back surfaces, we have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(y,z)=y^2+z^2$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(y,z)=2y.$\n\nTherefore, our type II representation is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:y^2+z^2 \\leq x \\leq 2y \\big\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: (y - 1)^2 +  z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6176.json" "file_sha256" "45e4028c159d10b4fb3561152013805cc004b4803ef14c652fe5e3aea360101e"} {"question" "e-6177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The region $R$ is bounded between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1.$ Express $R$ as a type III region in the form\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6177.json" "file_sha256" "83bbf4fe8855a233d79fa4785a02a05f0852e2e739aa5f718e17f99850daddaa"} {"question" "e-6177" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y.$ Equating the two expressions, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}+1 & =2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $x^2 +  2z^2 = 2$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: x^2 +  2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the left and right surfaces, we have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(x,z)=2x^2+z^2-1$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(x,z)=x^2-z^2+1.$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of our ellipse is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 2x^2+z^2-1 \\leq y \\leq x^2-z^2+1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: x^2 +  2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043025/example-6177.json" "file_sha256" "83bbf4fe8855a233d79fa4785a02a05f0852e2e739aa5f718e17f99850daddaa"}] "usage" {"e-6176" [] "q-73574" [] "e-6177" [] "e-6175" [] "q-160965" []} "basis" 1598 "retractions" [[17592186134424 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/q-73574.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is bounded by the planes $x = 0$, $x = 1$, $y = 0$, $y = 2$, $z = 0$, and $z = 4 - x - y$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x, y) ∈ D, f_{1}(x, y) \\le z \\le f_{2}(x, y)}$\nas a type I region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2}$\n2. $f_{1}(x, y) = 1$ and $f_{2}(x, y) = 4 - x - y$\n3. $f_{1}(x, y) = 0$ and $f_{2}(x, y) = 2$"] [17592186134047 :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and the plane $x = 2y$. Given its representation\n$R = {(x, y, z): a \\le y \\le b, g_{1}(y) \\le z \\le g_{2}(y), f_{1}(y, z) \\le x \\le f_{2}(y, z)}$\nas a type II region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{1}(y, z) = y^{2} + z^{2}$ and $f_{2}(y, z) = 2y$\n2. $g_{1}(y) = 0$ and $g_{2}(y) = 1 - y^{2}$\n3. $a =-1$ and $b = 1$"] [17592186133559 :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=2x$. Express $R$ as a type I region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: a \\leq x \\leq b, \\quad g_1(x) \\leq y \\leq g_2(x), \\quad f_1(x,y) \\leq z \\leq f_2(x,y) \\big\\}$."] [17592186133559 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/288733533249dbff97fbaa397a40cf7d.png)\n\nLet's find a type I representation of our region.\n\n**Step 1.** We start by finding the surfaces $f_1(x,y)$ and $f_2(x,y)$. Since all $z$ in the region are bounded *below* by\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand *above* by $z=2x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+y^2}_{f_1(x,y)} \\leq z \\leq \\underbrace{2x}_{f_2(x,y)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nz=x^2+y^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $z=2x$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $z$. Substituting this expression for $z$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + y^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x^{2} - 2x + 1) + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle\n\n$$\n(x - 1)^2 + y^2 = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/8d32364434de168b5a340ae87c60abfe.png)\n\nThe region is bounded *below* by\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nand bounded *above* by\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_1(x)} \\leq y \\leq \\underbrace{\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}}_{g_2(x)}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the bounds for $x$. From the diagram, $x$ is bounded *below* by $x=0$ and bounded *above* by $x=2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{0}_a \\leq x \\leq \\underbrace{2}_b\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $R$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\:\\:-\\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2} \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt{1-(x-1)^2}, \\:\\: x^2+y^2 \\leq z \\leq 2x\\right\\}\n$$"] [17592186133560 :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $x=y^2+z^2$ and the plane $x=2y$. Express $R$ as a type II region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(y,z) \\leq x \\leq f_2(y,z) \\big\\}$."] [17592186133560 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional region of type II can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/088e0da77c5d0a30086a8df52ecb4549.png)\n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\nx=y^2+z^2\n$$\n\nand the plane $x=2y$ onto $yz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $x$. Substituting this expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2y &= y^{2} + z^{2} \\\\\ny^{2} - 2y + z^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(y^{2} - 2y + 1) + z^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n(y - 1)^{2} + z^{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle\n\n$$\n(y - 1)^2 + z^2 = 1\n$$\n\ncentered at $(1,0)$ in the $yz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: (y - 1)^2 + z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the front and back surfaces, we have that $x$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(y,z)=y^2+z^2$, and\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(y,z)=2y$.\n\nTherefore, our type II representation is\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (y,z) \\in D, \\:y^2+z^2 \\leq x \\leq 2y \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: (y - 1)^2 + z^2 \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$"] [17592186133561 :question/problem "The region $R$ is bounded between the hyperbolic paraboloid $y=x^2-z^2+1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $y=2x^2+z^2-1$. Express $R$ as a type III region in the form\n$R = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: f_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq f_2(x,z) \\big\\}$."] [17592186133561 :question/worked-solution "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2130/Source/Images/d50619957a793233d5ffe4220a746bf7.png)\n\nFirst, we find the projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid\n\n$$\ny=x^2-z^2+1\n$$\n\nand the elliptic paraboloid\n\n$$\ny=2x^2+z^2-1\n$$\n\nonto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equations are already solved for $y$. Equating the two expressions, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - z^{2} + 1 &= 2x^{2} + z^{2} - 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2z^{2} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2z^2 = 2\n$$\n\ncentered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the interior part of the ellipse:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: x^2 + 2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor the left and right surfaces, we have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by $f_1(x,z)=2x^2+z^2-1$, and\n- bounded *above* by $f_2(x,z)=x^2-z^2+1$.\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of our ellipse is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: 2x^2+z^2-1 \\leq y \\leq x^2-z^2+1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,z) \\:: \\: x^2 + 2z^2 \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130846 17592186130847 17592186130848 17592186130849 17592186130850 17592186132987 17592186132988 17592186132989 17592186132990 17592186132991] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130845 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130845 :answer-field/id] [17592186130845 :answer-field/key] [17592186130845 :answer-field/type] [17592186132986 :answer-field/correct] [17592186132986 :answer-field/id] [17592186132986 :answer-field/key] [17592186132986 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
